The required area is shaded The point of intersection of the curves are $x^2=x \Rightarrow x(x-1)=0$ i.e. $O(0,0) and P(1,1)$ <img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fmht_cet%252FXwg7FViJEMMxSjwhKcaJ09t6HmYE8z4Mlkohk5QFWL8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FXwg7FViJEMMxSjwhKcaJ09t6HmYE8z4Mlkohk5QFWL8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fmht_cet%2FXwg7FViJEMMxSjwhKcaJ09t6HmYE8z4Mlkohk5QFWL8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media"><br> $\begin{aligned}& \therefore A=\int_0^1\left( x-x²\right) d x \\
& =\left[\frac{x^2}{2}\right]_0^1-\left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_0^1=\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{3}=\frac{1}{6}
\end{aligned}$

Required area is shaded.
$\begin{aligned}
\text{ Area } &=\int_{1}^{4} x^{3} d x=\left[\frac{x^{4}}{4}\right]_{1}^{4} \\
&=\frac{1}{4}\left[4^{4}-1\right]=\frac{1}{4}(256-1)=\frac{255}{4} \text{ sq. unit }
\end{aligned}$

Line $y=4$ meets the parabola $y^{2}=x$ at A\therefore \quad 16=x$ and A$ is $(16,4) \therefore$ Required area $\begin{array}{l}
=\int_{y=0}^{4} x d y \\
=\int_{y=0}^{4} y^{2} d y \\
=\left[y^{3} / 3\right]_{0}^{4} \\
=\frac{64}{3} \text{ sq units }
\end{array}$
